<Doc>
    <p>The profile lives in <code>src/profile</code>, at <code>/profile</code>.</p>

    <Section title="A field and its value are one copy">
        <Example
            id="crm-02-bind-value"
            app="crm-02"
            title="`bind:value` — the field writes the value, and the value fills the field"
            summary="A contact's email, with what a save would send. Type a new one."
            files={{
                'src/profile/lib/ContactFields.abide': contactFieldsSource,
                'src/profile/lib/ContactFields.test.ts': contactFieldsTestSource,
                'src/profile/lib/ContactFields.bench.ts': contactFieldsBenchSource,
            }}
        >
            <ContactFields />
        </Example>

        <p>The bench types into the field once it holds 100 characters, and once it holds 1,000.
            Its ratio is what a keystroke costs as the value grows: a keystroke writes the value once, and
            copies nothing.</p>

        <p><code>bind:value</code> sets the field from the value, and writes the value back on
            <code>input</code> or <code>change</code>. There is no form model beside it and no handler
            per field. A <code>&lt;textarea&gt;</code> and a <code>&lt;select&gt;</code> take the same
            binding.</p>

        <p>A value that starts as a number binds as one, and so does an
            <code>&lt;input type="number"&gt;</code> or a <code>type="range"</code>. Text that reads as
            a number writes the number, blank text writes <code>undefined</code>, and any other text
            writes nothing.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>You write</th><th>What it binds</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>bind:checked</code></td><td>a checkbox, to a boolean</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>bind:open</code></td><td>a <code>&lt;details&gt;</code>, to whether it is open</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>bind:group</code></td><td>radios to one value, or checkboxes to an array of their <code>value</code>s</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>bind:files</code></td><td>a file input, to an array of the files picked</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>{'bind:value={{get, set}}'}</code></td><td>a field to a pair you write, where the value is computed</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>{'bind:prop={state}'}</code></td><td>a component's prop, so the child's write reaches your value</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>An element's size, whether it is in view and where it sits are not values a field
            writes, so they are not binds. <code>observe</code> measures them, and
            <code>bind:element</code> takes what it hands back, one adaptor or a list of them.
            Each writes a reactive value you hand it, or calls a function you hand it instead.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>You write</th><th>What it measures</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>{'bind:element={observe.size(box)}'}</code></td><td>the element's content box, as <code>{'{ width, height }'}</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>{"observe.size(box, { box: 'border' })"}</code></td><td>the border box instead, padding and border in</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>{'bind:element={observe.visible(seen)}'}</code></td><td>whether the element is in the viewport</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>{'observe.visible(seen, { in: scroller })'}</code></td><td>whether it is in view inside an element you hold, such as a scroller</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>{'bind:element={observe.rect(placed)}'}</code></td><td>its rectangle in the viewport, as <code>getBoundingClientRect</code> reports it</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>{'bind:element={[observe.rect(placed), observe.size(box)]}'}</code></td><td>both, on one element</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>{'return observe.size(refresh)(node)'}</code></td><td>the size, from inside a <code>bind:element</code> function of your own</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>A measurement runs one way. The browser measures and writes the value, and nothing you
            write reaches the element. The first measurement lands after the first layout, so until
            then the value holds what you gave it. Every observed element shares one observer, and
            an element that leaves is no longer observed. The server has no layout and runs none of
            it.</p>

        <p>An adaptor is a function of the element that hands back what stops it, so a
            <code>bind:element</code> function of your own can call one on the node it is handed,
            beside the listeners it adds, and stop it with them. It shares the same one observer.</p>

        <p><code>in</code> takes an element, usually one <code>bind:element</code> gave you. Until
            it holds one, nothing is observed. The elements measured against one root share an
            observer, and that observer stops when the last of them leaves. <code>box</code> is
            <code>"content"</code> or <code>"border"</code>, and either box is written as
            <code>width</code> and <code>height</code> on screen, whatever the writing mode. Hand
            either option a reactive value and the element is measured again when it changes.</p>

        <p><code>observe.rect</code> reads again in the frame after the element resizes, the page or
            anything in it scrolls, the window resizes, or the element moves for any other reason,
            such as a sibling above growing: one read per element per frame, however many events
            arrived. Each observed element has an <code>IntersectionObserver</code> of its own, fitted
            to its last rectangle, which costs nothing while it stays put and is made again after
            each move. For a crosshair or a hover that follows the pointer, call
            <code>getBoundingClientRect()</code> in the event handler instead.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Where a reactive value's name is written decides what it reaches">
        <p><code>reactive</code> holds a value, and inside a <code>.abide</code> file its name
            reads and writes it. The sugar sits over two explicit members, <code>s</code> and
            <code>s.set</code>, and a <code>.ts</code> file writes those out.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>You write</th><th>You get</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>foo</code> wherever the declared type of its position is not a <code>Reactive</code></td><td>a live read</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>const x: Reactive&lt;…&gt; = foo</code> / a <code>Reactive&lt;…&gt;</code>-typed parameter, object literal member, return or <code>bind:</code> operand</td><td>the <code>Reactive</code> itself</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>const [a, b] = foo</code> / <code>...foo</code></td><td>one read, taken apart</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>const &#123; a, b: c &#125; = bar()</code> where a member is a <code>Reactive</code></td><td>each name read and written as that <code>Reactive</code>, as if declared on its own</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>foo = bar</code></td><td>a write</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>foo.bar</code></td><td>the value's <code>bar</code>, or the <code>Reactive</code>'s own <code>bar</code> where it declares one the value does not</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>foo.bar(…)</code></td><td>the value's method where it has one, and otherwise a member of the <code>Reactive</code> such as <code>busy</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>foo()</code> / <code>foo.set(v)</code></td><td>the explicit read and write</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>await foo</code></td><td>the value, once it has settled</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>refresh(foo)</code> / a <code>Selection</code>-typed parameter</td><td>the <code>Reactive</code> itself</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A transform shapes what the record keeps">
        <Example
            id="crm-02-transform"
            app="crm-02"
            title={'`reactive(v, { transform })` — the record keeps the digits'}
            summary="A phone number typed with punctuation. Type one in the usual way."
            files={{
                'src/profile/lib/PhoneField.abide': phoneFieldSource,
                'src/profile/lib/PhoneField.test.ts': phoneFieldTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <PhoneField />
        </Example>

        <p>The transform runs on every write, so the field shows back what was stored rather than
            what you typed. <code>Accepted</code> is the type a write takes and <code>Stored</code> is
            the type the transform returns. <code>Shaped</code> is everything a transform can return,
            a refusal included, and <code>Transformer</code> is the type of the option.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A derived value follows the fields it read">
        <Example
            id="crm-02-derived"
            app="crm-02"
            title="`reactive(() => …)` — initials and a count, computed from the fields"
            summary="A profile's initials and how much of it is filled in. Change the name, then add a phone."
            files={{
                'src/profile/lib/ProfileSummary.abide': profileSummarySource,
                'src/profile/lib/ProfileSummary.test.ts': profileSummaryTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <ProfileSummary />
        </Example>

        <p>Give <code>reactive</code> a body, <code>reactive(() =&gt; …)</code>, and what the body
            read on its last run is what reruns it. You list no dependencies. The test counts the work:
            a phone edit walks to the count and never reaches the initials, because the initials never
            read the phone.</p>

        <p>A body with a parameter, <code>reactive((args) =&gt; …)</code>, is a
            <code>Keyed</code>: a factory holding one value per set of arguments. See
            <a href={url('/guide/keep-it-current')}>Keep it current</a>, where the pipeline keeps one
            per stage.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A write through a path copies down the path">
        <Example
            id="crm-02-path-write"
            app="crm-02"
            title={'`bind:value={contact.address.city}` — a write two levels down'}
            summary="A contact's city, inside its address. Type a new city."
            files={{
                'src/profile/lib/AddressField.abide': addressFieldSource,
                'src/profile/lib/AddressField.test.ts': addressFieldTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <AddressField />
        </Example>

        <p>The binding writes through the path, the way <code>contact.address.city = city</code>
            would: it copies <code>contact</code>, copies its <code>address</code>, sets
            <code>city</code>, and writes the new record back. The copy wakes the readers. A record
            changed in place is the object it already was, and nothing downstream has a reason to
            read it again.</p>

        <p><code>foo.bar = v</code> is that write wherever you spell it, and so is
            <code>foo.push(v)</code>: where the value is an array, <code>tags.push(tag)</code> runs on a
            copy and writes the copy back. The array's type decides it, so a class's own
            <code>push</code> stays the class's call.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="An expression in the markup follows what it reads">
        <p>Put <code>{'{}'}</code> around an expression and it updates the one thing it lands
            on, and nothing around it.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>You write</th><th>Where it lands</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>{'{expr}'}</code></td><td>a text node, escaped</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>{'{raw(...)}'}</code></td><td>markup, unescaped and unchecked (32.77): <code>raw</code> brands a string as a <code>Raw</code>, so hand it markup you wrote, never a model's reply or a fetched page as it came</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>{'name={expr}'}</code></td><td>one attribute, whole</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>{'name="…{expr}…"'}</code></td><td>one attribute, interpolated</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>{'class:name={cond}'}</code></td><td>one class, there while the condition holds</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>{'style:prop={value}'}</code></td><td>one style property</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>{'{...expr}'}</code></td><td>every attribute on an element, or every prop on a component</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>{'on<event>={fn}'}</code></td><td>a native listener, removed with its element</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>{'bind:element={Reactive<Element> | ((element: Element) => void | Disposer) | ((element: Element) => void | Disposer)[]}'}</code></td><td>the element itself, to focus or measure, or a list of callbacks each handed it</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A keyed list removes one row and leaves the rest">
        <Example
            id="crm-02-keyed-list"
            app="crm-02"
            title={'`{#for tag of tags by tag}` — a row per tag, kept by its key'}
            summary="A contact's tags. Remove one."
            files={{
                'src/profile/lib/Tags.abide': tagsSource,
                'src/profile/lib/Tags.test.ts': tagsTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <Tags />
        </Example>

        <p><code>{'{#for item, index of list by key}'}</code> renders its body once per item, and
            <code>by</code> says which row is which. The test removes the first tag and counts the
            work: one row goes, and no other row's text is touched. Without <code>by</code> the rows
            are matched by position, so the two that stay are both rewritten.</p>

        <p>A list shown in more than one place can carry its key on the value instead:
            <code>{"reactive(contacts, { by: 'id' })"}</code>.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A branch is in the page only while its condition holds">
        <Example
            id="crm-02-conditional"
            app="crm-02"
            title={'`{#if}` — a date field that exists only when asked for'}
            summary="A follow-up reminder for this contact. Tick the box, then clear it."
            files={{
                'src/profile/lib/FollowUp.abide': followUpSource,
                'src/profile/lib/FollowUp.test.ts': followUpTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <FollowUp />
        </Example>

        <p><code>{'{#if cond}'}</code> builds the branch it takes and nothing of the one it does
            not. Clearing the box removes the date field, and an <code>{'{:else}'}</code> branch would
            render in its place. <code>{'{#try}'}</code> is the other block: it renders <code>{'{:catch e}'}</code>
            in place of its whole body when rendering the body throws.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A component reads its props live">
        <Example
            id="crm-02-props"
            app="crm-02"
            title="`props()` — the avatar follows the name it was handed"
            summary="A contact card with an avatar component. Change the name."
            files={{
                'src/profile/lib/ContactCard.abide': contactCardSource,
                'src/profile/lib/Avatar.abide': avatarSource,
                'src/profile/lib/ContactCard.test.ts': contactCardTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <ContactCard />
        </Example>

        <p>A component is a <code>.abide</code> file, and <code>&lt;Name/&gt;</code> invokes it.
            <code>props</code> hands back each prop as a live read of what the caller passed, so the
            avatar follows the field and nothing is rebuilt: the test counts no node made or moved.</p>

        <p>A prop the child only reads holds the caller's <code>Reactive</code> wherever a name
            would: handed on to a <code>bind:</code> or a <code>Reactive</code>-typed parameter, it
            passes the <code>Reactive</code> itself. Its probes answer for everything the caller's expression read, so
            <code>busy(rows)</code> in a table component is busy while the list its page passed
            loads. <a href={url('/guide/talk-to-the-server')}>Talk to the server</a> introduces the
            probes.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>What it does for the CRM</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>&lt;Tag&gt;…&lt;/Tag&gt;</code></td><td>passes the markup between the tags as <code>children</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>&lt;slot&gt;fallback&lt;/slot&gt;</code></td><td>renders the children, or the fallback when there are none</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>{'{#component Name(pattern)}'}</code></td><td>declares a component inline, for a row worth naming but not a file</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>Attributes&lt;Tag&gt;</code></td><td>types the props a wrapper passes on to its element</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>**/node_modules/**/*.abide</code></td><td>a design system's components, compiled with the app that imports them</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </Section>

    <Section title="An edit to a copy of a prop stands until the prop changes">
        <Example
            id="crm-02-prop-draft"
            app="crm-02"
            title={'`reactive(() => name)` — a draft that starts over with each contact'}
            summary="A display name field under two contacts. Edit Grace's, then open Ada."
            files={{
                'src/profile/lib/ContactRename.abide': contactRenameSource,
                'src/profile/lib/NameDraft.abide': nameDraftSource,
                'src/profile/lib/ContactRename.test.ts': contactRenameTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <ContactRename />
        </Example>

        <p>The field is bound to <code>draft</code>, a derived value over the <code>name</code> prop.
            Typing writes the draft, and the write stands until the body runs again (11.8), which it
            does when <code>name</code> changes. So opening Ada starts the draft over from her name,
            and the component stays: the test counts no node made or moved.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A one-item keyed loop builds its markup again">
        <Example
            id="crm-02-fresh-subtree"
            app="crm-02"
            title={'`{#for shown of [open] by shown}` — a panel built fresh per contact'}
            summary="A contact panel that fades in. Open Ada, then Grace."
            files={{
                'src/profile/lib/ContactPanel.abide': contactPanelSource,
                'src/profile/lib/ContactPanel.test.ts': contactPanelTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <ContactPanel />
        </Example>

        <p>A component reads its props live, so a new contact rewrites the panel in place, and a
            fade-in on its first paint never plays again. A loop over a one-item list keyed by the
            contact builds a new row whenever the key changes, and the test finds a new element where
            the old one was. Reach for it where something only a new element resets has to start
            over: an animation, focus, an uncontrolled field. State held in reactive values is the
            card above, and needs no rebuild.</p>

        <p>A page is the same case. Navigating to the page already shown, with other
            <code>route.params</code>, keeps the page (30.23), so
            <code>{'{#for each of [route.params.id] by each}'}</code> is how part of it is built again.
            <a href={url('/guide/add-pages')}>Add pages</a> introduces the params.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A component's styles reach only its own markup">
        <Example
            id="crm-02-scoped-style"
            app="crm-02"
            title="`<style>` — two components, one class name, two rules"
            summary="A lead badge and a customer badge, side by side. Each styles its own badge class in its own color."
            files={{
                'src/profile/lib/StageBadges.abide': stageBadgesSource,
                'src/profile/lib/LeadBadge.abide': leadBadgeSource,
                'src/profile/lib/CustomerBadge.abide': customerBadgeSource,
                'src/profile/lib/StageBadges.test.ts': stageBadgesTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <StageBadges />
        </Example>

        <p>A <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> block's rules require the component's scope token, and only
            that component's markup carries it. So <code>.badge</code> in one file never reaches the
            other's badge, and neither needs a naming convention.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>What it does for the CRM</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>:global(…)</code></td><td>exempts one selector from the scope</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>import './app.css'</code></td><td>makes the app's stylesheet a dependency of the component that imports it</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>bunfig.toml</code></td><td>declares a bun plugin, such as Tailwind, that builds the stylesheet</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A script runs per instance, and a module script once">
        <Example
            id="crm-02-script"
            app="crm-02"
            title="`<script>` and `<script module>` — per row, and once"
            summary="Two contact rows from one component. Log a call on the first."
            files={{
                'src/profile/lib/CallLog.abide': callLogSource,
                'src/profile/lib/CallRow.abide': callRowSource,
                'src/profile/lib/CallLog.test.ts': callLogTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <CallLog />
        </Example>

        <p>A <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> block runs each time the component is used, top to
            bottom, so each row has its own <code>calls</code>. A <code>&lt;script module&gt;</code>
            block runs once for the file, so both rows count up one <code>opened</code>. On a server, a
            <code>reactive</code> in module scope is still one per request.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Next">
        <ul>
            <li><a href={url('/guide/talk-to-the-server')}>Talk to the server</a> — read deals from
                the server, change them, refuse a bad request, and say why</li>
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Doc>
